title: Everyone Wants to Design a Chair
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

A chair is just a chair, but they can also be so much more. They're not just a place to rest or work or eat or drink. Chairs are perhaps the most ubiquitous and important design element in our environments. 

Every designer wants to design a chair. Mies said it plainly: a chair is harder than a skyscraper. So the temptation, always, is to make something notable — a new idea, a new material, a new silhouette that gets your name attached to it forever.

These aren't those chairs. We've made 16 so far, one per artist per week, built in a single session from whatever was left in the shop. None will be a design classic. That's not a failure of ambition. A notable chair perhaps starts to answer "why a chair." These many un-notable ones, made under the same adhocist constraints week after week, start to provide clear answers as to "what is a chair" …

The chair has a unique level of interaction. It allows a physical connection with its user, and can engender an emotional response. People get emotionally attached to chairs that are comfortable, or chairs that belonged to someone important in their life. Chairs follow the human body — they cradle and support it. 
 
Everyone has a favorite chair. 

Chairs are mirrors of the wider cultural context of the eras they're produced within. A rich, complex tapestry of changing taste, materials, technologies, and ideologies. Nothing is constant in chairs other than that they offer an invitation to repose. I don't know what that means about the chairs from this project, but I see them fitting into [[mine|Kristian Bjornard]] and [[Chris's|Chris Attenborough]] art and design practice. 

Chairs have become almost totemic objects of desire. Sometimes they fall into the world of sculpture over function. Does that mean they're less of a chair?
 
Do you have a favorite chair? One of mine is the Chieftain Chair by Finn Juhl. As well as the Eames lounge chair — we had a knockoff of it when I was a kid. 
 
When you think about contemporary chairs, you can probably go back to Thonet in Vienna, and his work figuring out how to mass produce and flat-pack and ship chairs all over Europe and then into the United States. 
 
When you're making a chair, should you be thinking about ergonomics? about comfort? How do we deal with accessibility — the fact that people of different heights probably prefer different chair seat rises and angles, different leg lengths prefer different seat depths? There's no one right chair for everyone. 

There have been numerous notable chair designs, and I'm often fond of them. Presumably none of ours will be notable chair designs — they'll be utilitarian, pragmatic, ad hoc chair designs that should reward and entertain the user.
 
Some of our chair's in this project are inspired by famous designers, like Gerrit Rietveld or Enzo Mari. Some are purely inspired by a particular piece of wood or object found that week.

These are chairs that, perhaps aren't notable chair designs, but may be inspired by notable chair design. Chairs that might never be a modern design classic. They're meant to be functional, serviceable furniture.
  
